<spell-notebook "action usage">
	<header level="1">
		Action
		<header>An action is a menu item or toolbar button drawn from something a program can do:  its title, icon and shortcut keys, greyed while it can't be done.</>
	</>
	<action-tab-bar showing="usage"/>
	<section>
		<menu class="ui-docs-section-tabs" appearance="segmented" aria-label="Sections">
			<item link="#using-it">Using it</>
			<item link="#keyboard">Keyboard</>
			<item link="#accessibility">Accessibility</>
		</>
	</>

	<!-- USAGE:  how to use it (attributes in practice, events, framework snippets), keyboard, accessibility. -->
	<section label="Using it">
		<markdown>
			Put `<ui-action>`s where you'd put `<ui-item>`s:  in a `<ui-menu>`, a `<ui-toolbar>` or a list.  Each draws as one of its items.

			- Written by hand:  its title as its text, its keys in `shortcut`, `icon`, `disabled` and `selected`.
			- Drawn from an action:  set its `action` property, and it reads the title, icon, shortcut and state from there, live.  A click runs it, unless it's disabled.
			- Any object of the action's shape will do;  Spell's runner hands it each item of a template's menu.
		</>
		<markdown>
			```js
			const undo = document.createElement("ui-action")
			undo.action = {
			  get title() { return history.undoTitle },  // "Undo Turn Card"
			  shortcut: "mod+Z",
			  get isEnabled() { return history.canUndo },
			  isSelected: false,
			  run: () => history.undo()
			}
			document.querySelector("ui-menu").append(undo)
			```
		</>
	</>

	<section label="Keyboard">
		<markdown>As its menu's or toolbar's items:  Tab, or the arrow keys in an `interactive` menu, reach it;  Enter or Space runs it.  Its `shortcut` is only drawn:  the page binds the keys (Spell's runner does, for a program's actions).</>
	</>

	<section label="Accessibility">
		<markdown>
			A `<button>` (a link with `link`), named by its title;  `aria-disabled` while it can't be done, the owner's role in a menubar (`menuitem`).  The key caps are part of its name as a screen reader reads it, after the title.
		</>
	</>
</spell-notebook>
